--TEST--
every `Core` parameter is callable by the spec's `$name` — a static and an instance member written out of order, a defaulted positional skipped in the middle, the trailing bag as `options:`, and written order as evaluation order (ADR 0063 R2, `rule:types/arrays`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Tap {
    // Written order is observable: each of these says when it ran.
    public static function text(string $mark, string $v): string {
        echo "tap:", $mark, " ";
        return $v;
    }
    public static function count(string $mark, uint $v): uint {
        echo "tap:", $mark, " ";
        return $v;
    }
}

// The names are `docs/spec/01-core-library.md`'s signature column, so which
// parameter an argument fills is its name and not its place in the list.
echo Core\Str::repeat(times: 3, s: "ab"), "\n";

// A defaulted positional skipped in the *middle*: `$padding` takes the
// registry row's own default, which is the registry's and not the call's.
echo "[", Core\Str::padStart(length: 4, s: "x"), "]\n";

// R2's trailing bag is callable by the one name every bag has, whatever the
// member is — `options`, never a per-member spelling.
array<int> $odd = Core\Arr::range(1, 5, options: {step: 2});
echo Core\Arr::count($odd), "\n";

// An instance member takes the same rule; the receiver is not a parameter and
// so is not one of the names.
Core\Uri $base = Core\Uri::parse("https://example.test/a/b");
echo $base->resolve(reference: "c")->toString(), "\n";

// Evaluation is in written order; placement is in parameter order. The two
// disagree here, and both are visible on one line.
echo Core\Str::repeat(times: Tap::count("times", 2), s: Tap::text("s", "z")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
ababab
[   x]
3
https://example.test/a/c
tap:times tap:s zz
